                "title": "Ebola surge, AI math shocker and daylight saving time",
                "name": "Ebola surge, AI math shocker and daylight saving time",
                "podcast": {
                    "id": "1",
                    "name": "60-Second Science"
                },
                "description": "In this week&rsquo;s news roundup, we take a look at what&rsquo;s happening with the Ebola outbreak in parts of Africa. Later SciAm editorial fellow Peter Hall joins host Rachel Feltman to discuss recent claims by OpenAI and Anthropic that their models have solved some of the world&rsquo;s toughest math and physics problems. Plus, we dive into new research that suggests making daylight saving time permanent could put schoolchildren at risk.\nRecommended Reading:\nDueling proofs from OpenAI and Anthropic astound scientists\nOpenAI just scooped mathematicians&rsquo; work on another million-dollar problem\nE-mail us at sciencequickly@sciam.com if you have any questions, comments or ideas for stories we should cover!\nDiscover something new every day: subscribe to Scientific American and sign up for Today in Science, our daily newsletter.\nScience Quickly is produced by Rachel Feltman, Fonda Mwangi, Sushmita Pathak, Allison Rodgers and Jeff DelViscio. This episode was edited by Alex Sugiura, with fact-checking by Marielle Issa and Aaron Shattuck. Our theme music was composed by Dominic Smith.\nLearn more about your ad choices. Visit megaphone.fm\/adchoices",
