                    "id": "1",
                    "name": "60-Second Science"
                },
                "description": "What does science have to do with the midterms? In this episode of Science Quickly, host Rachel Feltman speaks with Scientific American breaking news reporter Adam Kovac about candidates with science backgrounds, the future of Medicare for All, the role of evidence in policymaking and the election&rsquo;s consequences for the broader scientific community.\nRecommended Reading:\nAbdul El-Sayed explains how science influences his politics\nTom Stalcup on climate change, a science-based approach to politics and misinformation\nE-mail us at sciencequickly@sciam.com if you have any questions, comments or ideas for stories we should cover!\nDiscover something new every day: subscribe to Scientific American and sign up for Today in Science, our daily newsletter.\nScience Quickly is produced by Rachel Feltman, Fonda Mwangi, Sushmita Pathak, Allison Rodgers and Jeff DelViscio. This episode was edited by Alex Sugiura, with fact-checking by Marielle Issa and Aaron Shattuck. Our theme music was composed by Dominic Smith.\nLearn more about your ad choices. Visit megaphone.fm\/adchoices",
